{
  "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
  "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "David S. Mann",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are four points I would like to make this morning. First, the plain language of Exemption 2 dictates an extremely narrow category of materials, those related solely to internal personnel policies and rules."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could I ask you this? The D.C. Circuit's decision in Crooker has been the leading decision in this -- on this issue for nearly 30 years, and there has been a great deal of reliance on it, certainly by the lower courts in general, and perhaps also by Congress and by the executive branch."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David S. Mann",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, Your Honor. When Crooker came down, the court was faced with an extremely -- a circumstance dealing with these FBI or ATF search and seizure manuals, and the court adopted and created the High 2 exemption."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why does that show that? I mean, why doesn't it show acceptance of Crooker?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David S. Mann",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First of all, Congress originally did look at amending section 2 -- Exemption 2, at the same time they looked at amending Exemption 7. They chose not to."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE BREYER",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0001-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "positions of those munitions in order to make them\nunsafe, for example, blowing them up. So that seemed\nlike a very serious circumvention of what the regulation\nwas there for."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. YANG",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioner has asked this Court to disrupt\n30 years of FOIA practice by rejecting an interpretation\nof Exemption 2 that has prevailed and has provided a\nworkable standard for agencies and the courts since the\nD.C. Circuit's en banc decision in Crooker."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0001-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ANTHONY A. YANG\nON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. YANG",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I think the -- as our brief\nexplained, there are three basic elements to Exemption\n2. The first is the internal element, and we believe\nthat means that the records at issue must be properly\nmaintained within an agency and not for general release."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE GINSBURG",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Yang, but the\ninformation about location I thought was disclosed,\nwhere these explosives were stored."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. YANG",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0001-0007",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "that was shared in confidence with these local force\nresponders and was unfortunately disclosed without our\nauthorization."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE SCALIA",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "All the rules of an agency\nwould -- would be sucked in, wouldn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. YANG",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. YANG: Well, no, I think what it did is it took Crooker as understood, but what it did is with respect to law enforcement records -- and remember it's only records compiled for law enforcement purposes that would disclose techniques, procedures, or guidelines for investigations or in prosecutions. In that context, Congress lowered the bar. It provided more protection for those law enforcement records than Crooker did."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: What happens -- I'm just curious -- on -- if you classify -- suppose you have a document, \"in case of emergency, these are the evacuation procedures\" of a big Federal building. And now you want to show that to the firemen, but you don't want it to be in the newspaper. All right. And the firemen don't have classifications, and they aren't cleared."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c903e32b70e2-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Do you think it's practical to classify all of the information that might have security implications?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1163",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "c903e32b70e2d26c4f32a548fb53d0218db69e42beddefeb564a29dcd6515faf",
